Runtime on ordinary x86_64 Hyper-V (not CoCo):

  The exact pinned bytes boot as a Gen2 guest with the candidate symbols
  present. The lifecycle half is what repeated. On all eleven runs, on
  both guests, the vmbus_alloc_buffer map set returns to its exact
  baseline across 100 hv_netvsc rebind cycles: PHASE1-BEFORE and
  PHASE1-AFTER both read MAPS count=12 bytes=20279296 pages=4939, with
  zero splats and zero faults, and LIFECYCLE_VERDICT=PASS cycles=100
  phase2=yes every time. Intermediate samples taken during a rebind
  show the in-flight maps. Four of the twenty-two guest-runs differ
  from the other eighteen at those checkpoints. On 36972315260's
  windows-2025 guest they read count=18 bytes=73887744 pages=18021
  at every ten-cycle checkpoint and 17 at cycle 100 -- flat, not
  growing. On 36975012655's windows-2025 guest they read 12 at
  every checkpoint except cycle 50, which reads count=11
  bytes=19226624 pages=4683 -- a one-sample dip, not a trend.
  36978224340's guests are not flat at all: windows-latest samples
  13, 11, 18, 18, 12, 15, 18, 18, 12 and 18 across the ten
  checkpoints, and windows-2025 samples 12 except for 18 at cycle 60
  and 11 at cycle 80. In-flight maps during a rebind are transient
  state, not a gate. The checkpoint lines are what the gate reads,
  and PHASE1-AFTER matches PHASE1-BEFORE exactly on every one.

  The mmap-versus-release window repeats the same way. With a userspace
  mapping held open across the teardown, the UIO device maps and the
  2 MiB subchannel ring both stay alive while the ring is freed
  (/dev/uio0 maps=5, sysfs ring maps=1, PHASE2 hold-in-mmap window
  OPEN) on every guest-run in the census above. The 100 bind/unbind
  cycles complete with zero bind failures (400 steps, cycle_fails=0).
  The exact map-set match is the PHASE1 pair above. PHASE2 allocates
  its own subchannel region and does not claim to return to BASELINE;
  FINAL reads one region above it, which is that region still live
  after restore_nic. The wrapper note below covers the counts taken
  outside these gates. No splat, fault, or accept4 failure appears on
  any of the twenty-two guest-runs.

  The sysfs ring map goes through hv_uio_ring_mmap(), which rejects a
  channel that is not CHANNEL_OPENED_STATE with -ENODEV. A subchannel
  can be present in sysfs and still read maps=0 if the helper reaches
  it before the open completes; the UIO device map carries the hold in
  that case. uio0 reads maps=5 on every guest-run in the census.

  One wrapper count sits outside both gates and differs on two
  guests. after_lifecycle, the harness map count taken after
  restore_nic, reads 14 against a baseline of 12 on 36972315260's
  windows-2025 guest and 9 on 36978224340's windows-latest guest,
  where the other twenty guest-runs read 13. Both readings are
  consistent with the restored NIC re-establishing a ring map after
  the FINAL snapshot, at different points in that window; that
  reading is an interpretation, not a measurement, and neither
  PHASE1 nor PHASE2 is affected.

  The 115200 serial capture also drops single bytes now and then,
  which is worth naming so a reader of the raw logs is not surprised
  by a corrupted word. 36975012655's windows-2025 guest prints its
  PHASE1-BEFORE line as MAPS count=12 byts=... with the e missing,
  and 36978224340's guests print HYPERV_DRIL_BOOT,
  HYPERV_DRIL_DEVICE and high_order_7plus_blocks_reread0, each with
  one character gone. Earlier runs lost a single character the same
  way. The numbers on those lines are intact and the harness reads
  the counts; no claim above is taken from a corrupted word.

  Fragmentation depth is the part that does not repeat, and the letter
  does not claim it does. The buddy-fragmentation drill allocates until
  /proc/buddyinfo holds no free block of order 7 or above, then punches
  physical buddy holes whose partners it keeps pinned, so nothing above
  order-0 can coalesce. Whether it reaches full depletion depends on
  how the 4096 hole pairs happen to land, and that varies per run:

