  So full order-7 depletion is reached in nineteen of twenty-two
  guest-runs. The drill reports INCONCLUSIVE_CAP_REACHED on the
  three that stop short, rather than claiming an exhaustion it did
  not measure. One of those three is the only guest-run in the set
  that the harness grades PARTIAL overall: 36975012655's
  windows-latest guest, whose RESULT line reads lifecycle=0
  fragment=3 splats=0 faults=0 with
  scope=lifecycle-pass-fragment-inconclusive. Its lifecycle half
  still passed; only the fragmentation half stopped short.

  What does repeat is the claim the series is about: channel open
  survives. On all twenty-two guest-runs the hv_netvsc rebind comes
  back (nic_rebound=yes), with zero accept4 failures and zero oops,
  at every measured depth -- including the three that stopped with a
  single order-7 block still free. In those twenty-two, where
  depletion was reached the verdict is
  PASS_RING_ALLOCATION_UNDER_FRAGMENTATION, scoped as
  ordinary-x86_64-vzalloc-path; co-co-chunked-fallback-not-exercised.

  Two runs outside that census name the wall past depletion. After
  the hog is mlocked, compaction is off and min_free_kbytes is
  forced to 512, a later channel open can die in SLUB before the
  ring allocator is reached at all. The oom-killer header on both
  is the same:

    gfp_mask=0x40cc0(GFP_KERNEL|__GFP_COMP), order=1
    Workqueue: hv_sub_chan vmbus_add_channel_work
    Out of memory and no killable processes...
    Kernel panic - not syncing: System is deadlocked on memory

  36982941081 and 37043082886, both windows-2025, both print
  PRE-OPEN 12 maps and then this at 4.1s of guest time, and neither
  reaches POST-OPEN. 0011 and 0012 have already taken the order-7
  requestor array and the order-2 RNDIS descriptor off this path;
  what is left is an ordinary SLUB new-slab page for a small
  __kmalloc, and with the hog holding about 1.9 GiB unevictable and
  init the only killable task, nothing can satisfy it. The buddy
  counters do not predict that. 36949824155 survived a tighter
  reading -- 2091 order-0 blocks and no order-1 at all -- than the
  two that died at 1238 and 2068 order-0 with four and five order-1
  still free at ready=1. What decides it is whether the kmalloc
  cache that open needs still has a free object, which the drill
  does not measure. Neither run is a ring-allocation failure, and
  neither is counted in the census above.

  On an ordinary x86_64 guest vmbus_alloc_buffer() always takes
  vzalloc(), so the chunked order-N -> order-0 degrade is not what ran
  here; that path is covered by fault injection and stays open on CoCo
  hardware.

  The drill's post-ready path is fork-free. After ready=1 every parse,
  every buddy and map count, and the channel rebind run on shell
  builtins until the helper is torn down. That constraint is
  load-bearing regardless of the numbers a given run reports: a munmap
  of 16 KiB frees four order-0 pages into the per-cpu page cache rather
  than one order-2 buddy block, drain_all_pages() never runs against a
  fully mlocked pattern, and earlier runs lost the shell and the
  console to a clone at exactly that moment. So the helper's
  groups=504 pages=2016 are a munmap tally, not forkable stacks, and
  buddy_order2= is reported from the buddy's own order-2-and-above
  count for information -- 0, 4, 5, 286, 305, 333, 339, 342, 362,
  and 466 have all been seen depending on the run, and it is not
  what the post-ready path trusts.

  A green drill job is not by itself a gate closure -- the gate is the
  RESULT, VERDICT and MMAP_HOLD lines in the guest console. The
  converse also holds: a hosted runner that starves one guest reddens
  the job with a clean fragmentation half and a lifecycle that never
  reaches cycle 10, which is a failed measurement rather than a code
  failure. Both halves are read from the console before a run is
  called anything.

  An earlier run against a build that still carried a second high-order
  allocation on the subchannel-open path -- the RNDIS request
  descriptor, fixed in 8/9 -- reached a buddy with buddy_order2=1 and
  died in netvsc_subchan_work -> get_rndis_request with "page
  allocation failure: order:2" and an oom-kill of the drill shell. In
  the census above the runs reach a tighter buddy than that
  (buddy_order2=0 on 36949824155) and the subchannel open survived.
  That is the failure mode 0012 removes, shown absent on the bytes
  that remove it. The SLUB wall named further up is a different
  failure mode, and is not what 0012 addresses.

  A later run against the same eight-patch bytes failed differently,
  and 9/9 is the response. Run 36960219285 panicked in netvsc_poll
  with "BUG: kernel NULL pointer dereference, address: 0000000000000000"
  and "Kernel panic - not syncing: Fatal exception in interrupt". A
  control RNDIS send registers a NULL skb as the request address, an
  empty completion for it resolved to that NULL address, and the
  unguarded empty-payload branch of netvsc_send_completion()
  dereferenced it. The crash is pre-existing relative to commit
  93f51579e7df ("Linux 7.3-rc4") and is not introduced by these diffs;
  the series exposes it because channel open now survives the
  fragmentation the drill creates. That run is cited here as a failure
  only.

  Patch 9/9 is the fix. Run 36965808247 is the runtime proof that it
  holds, and runs 36970357645 and 36972315260 repeat it on the
  corrected 6/9 bytes. All three boot as a Gen2 guest on both hosted
  guests and drive the same fragmentation and lifecycle sequence that
  found the panic. Every guest on every one of those runs reports
  VERDICT=PASS_RING_ALLOCATION_UNDER_FRAGMENTATION with
  order7_dmesg=0 accept4_failures=0 oops=0 rebind=yes, and
  LIFECYCLE_VERDICT=PASS cycles=100 phase2=yes with
  lifecycle=0 fragment=0 splats=0 faults=0. There is no NULL
  dereference and no panic on any of them. Run 36975012655 adds the
  same negative on the same bytes with one guest short of depletion,
  and 36978224340 repeats it on the revised commit messages. Both
  runs report order7_dmesg=0 accept4_failures=0 oops=0 rebind=yes
  and LIFECYCLE_VERDICT=PASS cycles=100 phase2=yes on every guest.
  The crash was in the completion path for a control RNDIS send, and
  those sends are part of every one of the 100 rebind cycles, so
  this is the surface that failed in 36960219285, shown surviving.

  One observation from a different surface, which is not evidence
  about these thirteen patches. A WSL 6.18 development tree carrying
  this design -- a different base from 7.3-rc4 and a different code
  shape, folding buffer release into vmbus_free_buffer() rather
  than exporting vmbus_release_buffer() -- is installed on a daily
  WSL2 workstation. A post-boot capture on it read zero splats,
  zero order-N allocation failures, zero accept4 failures and no
  code WARNING or BUG, with the VMBus buffer symbols present and
  107 live vmbus_alloc_buffer regions. That is a snapshot under
  real daily use, not a soak, and it qualifies nothing about the
  series as submitted.

What is not proven here
-----------------------

CoCo page-state transitions. SEV-SNP, TDX, and Arm CCA memory-state
behaviour has not been observed on real confidential hardware. The
per-chunk decrypt design is what those platforms need, and the static
invariants are machine-checked in CI, but that is a negative proof about
this patch set, not platform evidence. Platform validation is follow-up
work and this series should be read with that gap open.

Note also that the chunked order-N -> order-0 degrade only runs when
vmbus_needs_shared_pages() is true: a non-confidential buffer on a
guest where hv_is_isolation_supported() or
cc_platform_has(CC_ATTR_GUEST_MEM_ENCRYPT) holds. An ordinary x86_64
guest always takes the single vzalloc() path inside
vmbus_alloc_buffer(), so the runtime numbers above speak to ring
lifecycle and fragmentation resilience, not to the confidential
fallback. That fallback is covered by the KUnit injection tests and
awaits platform validation.

The design follows Michael Kelley's review of the v1 series. Comments
welcome.

